---
title: Spaghetti and Meatballs
description: Beef, pork, and prosciutto meatballs for a crowd, roasted in the oven and then braised in the sauce.
tags: [pasta]
course: main
icons: [meat, tomato, pot-food]
cuisine: Italian
source:
  name: America's Test Kitchen
  url: https://www.americastestkitchen.com/recipes/6218-classic-spaghetti-and-meatballs-for-a-crowd
servings: 12
yield: 40%meatballs
time: 3h15m
why:
  - Roasting the meatballs on wire racks browns them evenly. It's also faster and cleaner.
  - Gelatin plumps the meatballs and makes them soft and rich.
  - Prosciutto adds extra meatiness, and the panko and buttermilk paste keeps the meat moist and tender.
  - Tomato juice replaces half of the crushed tomatoes so the sauce doesn't cook down too far during the hour in the oven.
---

= Meatballs

Set the oven racks in the lower-middle and upper-middle positions and heat the oven to 450°F (230°C). In a #large bowl{}, combine the @panko bread crumbs{170%g}(6 oz, about 2 1/4 cups) and @buttermilk{1 1/2%cup}. Let it sit, mashing now and then with a fork, until it becomes a smooth paste, about ~soak{10%minutes}.

> **No Buttermilk** — Use 1 cup of plain yogurt thinned with 1/2 cup of milk for every 1 1/2 cups of buttermilk.

Sprinkle the @powdered gelatin{1 1/2%tsp} over the @cold water{3%tbsp} in a #small bowl{} and let it stand for ~soften{5%minutes} to soften.

Add the @eggs{3}(large, lightly beaten), @ground beef{2%lb}(85 percent lean), @ground pork{1%lb}, @prosciutto{170%g}(6 oz, thinly sliced, chopped fine), @Parmesan cheese{85%g}(3 oz, about 1 1/2 cups, grated, plus more for serving), @fresh parsley{6%tbsp}(minced), @garlic cloves{3}(medium, minced), @table salt{1 1/2%tsp}, @black pepper{1/2%tsp}(plus more to taste), and the softened gelatin to the bread-crumb paste. Mix gently with your hands until everything is thoroughly combined.

Line two #rimmed baking sheets{} with foil, set a #wire rack{} in each, and coat the racks with nonstick cooking spray. Lightly shape about 1/4 cup of the mixture into a 2-inch ball (about 2 oz) and set it on a rack. Repeat with the rest of the mixture, spacing the meatballs evenly, to make about 40 meatballs.

Roast until the meatballs are browned, about ~roast{30%minutes}, swapping the sheets between racks and turning them front to back halfway through.

= Sauce

While the meatballs roast, heat the @extra-virgin olive oil{3%tbsp} in a large #Dutch oven{} over medium heat until shimmering. Add the @grated onion{1 1/2%cup}(from 1 to 2 onions, grated on the large holes of a box grater) and cook, stirring occasionally, until soft and golden at the edges, ~cook{6-8%minutes}.

Add the @&garlic cloves{6}, @red pepper flakes{1/2%tsp}, and @dried oregano{1%tsp} and cook, stirring often, until fragrant, about ~cook{30%seconds}.

Stir in the @tomato juice{6%cup}(bottled), @crushed tomatoes{3%can}(28 oz), @dry white wine{6%tbsp}, @&table salt{1 1/2%tsp}, and @&black pepper{} to taste. Raise the heat to medium-high and bring to a simmer, then lower the heat to medium-low and simmer until slightly thickened, about ~simmer{15%minutes}.

Take the meatballs out of the oven and lower the oven temperature to 300°F (150°C). Gently add the meatballs to the sauce, cover the pot, and put it in the oven on the lower-middle rack. Cook until the meatballs are firm and the sauce has thickened, about ~braise{1%hour}.

Meanwhile, bring 10 quarts of water to a boil in a 12-quart #pot{}. Add the @spaghetti{3%lb} and @&table salt{2%tbsp} and cook until al dente. Drain the pasta and return it to the pot.

= Serve

Stir the @fresh basil{1/2%cup}(minced) and @&fresh parsley{3%tbsp} into the sauce, then season to taste with @granulated sugar{}, salt, and pepper.

Toss the pasta with 1 1/2 cups of the sauce until lightly coated. Serve the pasta with the meatballs, the remaining sauce, and more grated Parmesan passed separately.

> **For Four** — Use one-third of every ingredient.
